# The 10 Skills That Help You Stand the F*ck Out

I'll be honest — I didn't set out to build a system.

I built El Mejor Coffee and made every positioning mistake you can make. I built Happy Beginnings and learned what it felt like to nail one. I helped founders and small businesses reposition themselves and watched the same problems appear over and over, just wearing different clothes.

What you're reading now is the distillation of that. Not a step-by-step tutorial — think of it more as a map. Here's what each tool does, where it belongs in your journey, and what it actually looks like when it works.

---

## 1. Brand Invisibility Audit

I used to think invisibility was a distribution problem. If the right people just saw us, they'd get it.

Wrong.

The first time I properly audited El Mejor's brand — really looked at the messaging, the visuals, the strategy side by side with everyone else in the coffee space — it was embarrassing. We were saying the same things. Using the same adjectives. Making the same promises in a slightly different font.

The Brand Invisibility Audit runs your brand across three dimensions: how you're communicating, how you're presenting visually, and how your overall strategy positions you. It scores each one. You get a number.

The number isn't the point. The patterns are the point. You walk away knowing exactly where you're blending in, so you know where to start.

Most founders never do this because it feels like a vanity exercise. It's not. It's diagnostics. You wouldn't try to fix a broken engine without knowing which part is failing.

---

## 2. Order Winners Finder

There's a concept in operations strategy — order winners vs. table stakes.

Table stakes are what you need just to be in the game. WiFi in a hotel. Decent onboarding in a SaaS product. Fast shipping from an e-commerce brand. Nobody chooses you because of these things. They just choose someone else if you don't have them.

Order winners are what make someone pick you over a credible alternative when all else is roughly equal.

When I was building El Mejor, I thought farmer welfare was an order winner. Customers cared about the environment, cared about ethics, wanted to feel good about their coffee purchase. Logical.

But the raving reviews told a different story. Customers were raving about feeling seen. About the personal touch. About the fact that the box had their name on it and told them the story of the farmer who grew their beans.

Farmer welfare was table stakes in the ethical coffee space. Personalisation was the order winner — the thing nobody else was doing, the thing that made people come back.

The Order Winners Finder forces you to draw that line clearly. What do you have to have? What do you uniquely win on? Most founders conflate the two and build their positioning on the wrong thing.

