## 4. Value Bucket Builder

People don't buy what you sell. They buy what they feel when they buy what you sell.

I learned this at El Mejor when I started paying real attention to why people were choosing us. Not the rational why — "good coffee, ethical sourcing" — but the actual emotional why.

For some customers, it was identity. Buying El Mejor said something about who they were: someone who cares about where things come from, who reads labels, who doesn't just default to the convenient option. That's social value — the story the purchase tells about you.

For others, it was the story itself. Aspiration. They weren't just buying coffee; they were participating in something meaningful.

The Value Bucket Builder maps your brand across four dimensions: functional (what it does), emotional (how it makes someone feel), social (what it signals to others), and aspirational (what it says about who they're becoming). Most brands are loud on functional, whisper on the rest.

The brands people genuinely love are fluent in all four. The brand promise that comes out of this work — "We help [target] achieve [functional] so they can [emotional/aspirational] without [friction]" — becomes the north star for everything you write.

